//! Bytecode interpreter: the bowser_js virtual machine.
//!
//! This module wires the existing pipeline stages together: lexer, parser,
//! compiler, and this interpreter, plus the event loop and the builtins.
//! `Vm::eval` parses, compiles, and runs a classic script; the interpreter
//! executes [`Op`][crate::opcode::Op] bytecode with lexical environments,
//! closures, exceptions, generators, async functions, and native re-entry.
//!
//! # Architecture
//!
//! ```text
//! source -> parse_script -> compile -> FuncTemplate
//!     -> interner (template <-> u32 id, shared by value)
//!     -> Interp::invoke -> Interp::run (the dispatch loop)
//! ```
//!
//! Compiled templates are interned once per `Vm` in [`Interp::funcs`] and
//! shared by value (`Rc<FuncTemplate>`), so every closure over the same
//! source function runs the same template. A [`Frame`] holds the per-call
//! state: program counter, operand-stack base, lexical environment, the
//! captured upvalue cells, the actual arguments, and the active exception
//! handlers.
//!
//! # Environments and closures
//!
//! Each frame owns a `Vec<EnvSlot>` with one slot per compiler binding id.
//! A slot is `Plain(Value)` until some nested closure captures it, at which
//! point it is boxed into `Shared(Rc<RefCell<Value>>)`; every later access
//! (local or through the cell) observes the same storage. `Op::MakeClosure`
//! resolves the template's [`UpvalueDesc`][crate::compiler::UpvalueDesc]
//! list: `Local(id)` boxes the current frame's slot `id` (found by name via
//! the template's `binding_names`), `Upvalue(i)` reuses the current frame's
//! own cell `i` (transitive capture).
//!
//! # Native re-entry
//!
//! Builtin natives receive `&mut Heap` but no VM handle. When a native
//! calls back into JavaScript (e.g. `Array.prototype.map` invoking its
//! callback), the shared choke point
//! [`call_function`][crate::builtin_object_array::call_function] delegates
//! bytecode callees to [`reenter_call`], which resumes the interpreter
//! through the [`ACTIVE`] thread-local. The interpreter state lives in
//! [`Interp`], disjoint from the [`Heap`], and every interpreter method
//! takes `heap: &mut Heap` explicitly, so the native's heap borrow and the
//! re-entrant interpreter borrow never alias.
//!
//! At most one `&mut Interp` derived from [`ACTIVE`] is ever live at a
//! time: `with_active` is the single derivation point, the borrow it hands
//! out cannot escape its closure, and every use nests strictly LIFO — an
//! inner re-entry's borrow always dies before any outer borrow is used
//! again. [`vm_call`] closures hold their borrow across arbitrary Rust
//! code, so they must never trigger a nested derivation while retaining
//! it (the promise constructor splits pre/post work into sequential
//! phases); [`reenter_call`] and [`reenter_construct_with_target`] fail
//! closed with a `TypeError` if one is attempted, rather than aliasing the
//! live borrow. The `new.target` observed by natives lives in its own
//! thread-local, so reading it needs no interpreter borrow at all.
//!
//! A JavaScript value thrown across the native/bytecode boundary travels as
//! [`NativeError::JsThrown`], so the VM can re-raise the original value
//! instead of a stringified copy.
//!
//! # Compiler/VM contracts
//!
//! * `#this`, `#newtarget`, `#argc` are VmProvided bindings: the VM
//!   pre-fills them by name on frame entry (arrows do not declare them and
//!   capture the enclosing ones lexically).
//! * Hidden intrinsics (`#arr_append`, `#construct`, …) are native functions
//!   held in [`Intrinsics::hidden`] and resolved by name in `load_name`
//!   before the global fallback, so user code can neither reach nor
//!   overwrite them through `globalThis`.
//! * `Op::Throw` (and VM-raised errors) interpret a thrown string of the
//!   form `"KindName: message"` as a construction request for that error
//!   kind; this is how compiler-emitted TDZ/const-assignment checks produce
//!   real `ReferenceError`/`TypeError` objects.
//! * `Op::IterNext` consumes the `[iterable, index]` pair the compiler
//!   builds (`for`-`of` pushes the value, `for`-`in` pushes the array from
//!   `#enum_keys`).
//!
//! # v1 gaps (documented, not silently wrong)
//!
//! * `Op::Yield`/`Op::Await` outside a generator/async frame trap.
//! * `for`-`of` over `Map`/`Set`/non-array iterables: `TypeError`.
//! * Thenable assimilation: only native promise objects are assimilated.
//! * `super` property access goes through `#super_construct` + prototype
//!   lookup; derived-class `new.target` wiring is best-effort.
//! * Class constructors have no call-guard marker in [`FuncTemplate`], so
//!   calling a class without `new` is not rejected (matches the compiler's
//!   current output).
//! * Automatic GC is disabled (the engine default); the interpreter never
//!   enables it, so frame/stack values need no extra rooting.
//! * Fuel is decremented once per dispatched op; deeply nested
//!   native->bytecode->native chains share one frame budget
//!   ([`Interp::max_frames`]).
//!
//! # Safety
//!
//! `with_active` is the only place that derives `&mut Interp` from the
//! [`ACTIVE`] raw pointer. The pointer is set by [`Interp::call_native`]
//! immediately before the native runs and cleared when the native returns;
//! while it is set, the interpreter is reached only through `with_active`,
//! whose borrows nest strictly LIFO (an inner borrow always dies before
//! any outer borrow is used again), so no two derived borrows ever
//! overlap. The primary `&mut Interp` rooted at `&mut Vm` is suspended
//! for the native's duration and resumes only after [`ACTIVE`] is
//! cleared.

